    <header>
        <h1>Kanchipuram: The Silk & Sacred Core</h1>
    </header>

    <section>
        <p>When you head into Kanchipuram, you’re stepping into a place where people have been coming for centuries to find a peace and perspective. When you leave, you usually carry the grace and blessings from the temples, and a few shopping bags filled with world-class silk and traditional snacks.</p>
        <p>Kanchipuram has two sides to it - especially the economy: the Micro-Economy of the Heritage Core, which functions on craftsmanship and religious tourism, and the Macro-Economy of the Industrial Corridor, which drives India’s global manufacturing output.<p>
	<p>To understand this ‘Temple & Tech’ district, we split Kanchipuram into its spiritual capital and the primary industrial hub.</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section>
        <h2>Spiritual Kanchipuram / கோவில் காஞ்சிபுரம்</h2>
        <p>In the city center, the economy is still largely tied to the medieval "Nagaram" (market center) model. Business here is conducted in dense, walkable streets where living and working spaces often overlap.</p>

        <h3>Shiva Kanchi / சிவ காஞ்சி</h3>
        <p>The western heart of Kanchipuram, defined by massive sandstone monuments, ancient Pallava architecture, and the sacred headquarters of Shakti worship. This is the western and most bustling part of the city. The narrow, 12-to-20-foot wide streets designed for foot traffic and small carts.</p>
        
        <h4>Key Temples:</h4>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Ekambareswarar Temple:</strong> Representing the element of Earth, this is the city's largest temple. It features a 3,500-year-old mango tree whose four branches are said to represent the four Vedas, and a massive 1,000-pillar hall built by Vijayanagara kings. The Mada Streets around Ekambareswarar are the commercial arteries.</li>
            <li><strong>Kailasanathar Temple:</strong> The oldest structural temple in the city (8th century). Built by the Pallavas using sandstone, it is a masterpiece of early Dravidian architecture, famous for its intricate relief sculptures and 58 small shrines encircling the main sanctum.</li>
            <li><strong>Kumarakottam Temple:</strong> Dedicated to Lord Murugan, it is strategically located between the Ekambareswarar and Kamakshi temples, completing the Somaskanda formation (Shiva, Parvati, and Child).</li>
            <li><strong>Kamakshi Amman Temple:</strong> One of the 51 Shakti Peethas. Uniquely, the deity is in a seated Padmasana posture. It is the only major temple in the city center where the goddess is the primary deity, with no separate Parvati shrines in nearby Shiva temples.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Economy & Business Hub:</h4>
        <p>The Economic Driver is the handloom silk weaving and dyeing. This is the primary cluster for the Saliyar and Sengunthar weaving communities. The business node here consists of cooperative societies and family-run ‘silk showrooms.’<p>
	<p>Unlike modern retail, much of the high-value trade happens in the backstreets where looms are active. This area serves as the primary business hub for the famous Kanchipuram silk weavers.<p>
	<p>Besides silk, a high-velocity retail zone specializing in ‘pooja’ materials, brass lamps, bronze icons, and traditional jewelry. Extreme high-density. Shops are often no more than 100-200 sq. ft., packed tightly around the temple's four gopurams.</p>

        <h3>Vishnu Kanchi / விஷ்ணு காஞ்சி</h3>
        <p>Located about 2 kilometers east, it is celebrated for its Vaishnavite complexes, golden lizard shrines, and deep connections to the Alvar saints. Vishnu Kanchi serves as a secondary silk hub but is quieter, focusing more on hospitality (lodges for pilgrims). The main economic drivers are heritage tourism and localized craft.</p>
        
        <h4>Key Temples:</h4>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Varadharaja Perumal Temple:</strong> It is a massive 23-acre complex featuring the famous ‘hundred-pillar hall’ with monolithic stone chains. The temple tank houses the Athi Varadar, the original deity, carved from fig wood, is kept in a silver casket underwater in the temple tank.</li>
	    </li>It is raised from the temple tank only once every 40 years (massive crowd). The last event was in 2019, making the next opportunity roughly 2059.</li>
            <li><strong>Vaikunta Perumal Temple:</strong> This temple is a Pallava-era marvel known for its unique three-tiered Vimana. Each tier houses Lord Vishnu in a different posture: standing on the ground floor, sitting on the first, and reclining on the second.</li>
            <li><strong>Ulagalantha Perumal Temple:</strong> Dedicated to the Vamana avatar (Trivikrama). The main deity is a colossal 35-foot-tall idol depicting Vishnu measuring the universe with one foot raised high.</li>
            <li><strong>Ashtabhuja Perumal Temple:</strong> Famous for depicting Lord Vishnu with eight arms, carrying eight different weapons to protect his devotees - a rare iconographic form in South India.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Jaina Kanchi / சமண காஞ்சி</h3>
        <p>Located on the southern bank of the Vegavathi River in a suburb called Tiruparuttikunram, this area preserves the city’s deep Jain history. It is often overlooked by the mainstream crowds and hence it is largely non-commercial.<p>
	<p>It marked a time when Kanchipuram was a premier center for Jain philosophy and art. It acts as an ‘academic’ and agricultural node on the city's outskirts.</p>
        
        <h4>Key Temples:</h4>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Trilokyanatha Temple:</strong> Also known as the Pallavamedu temple, it is the largest Jain temple in the region. It features the ‘Sangeetha Mandapa’ with 14th-century ceiling paintings that remain remarkably vivid, depicting the lives of various Tirthankaras.</li>
            <li><strong>Chandraprabha Temple:</strong> Located within the same complex as Trilokyanatha, this two-story structure is dedicated to the 8th Tirthankara. It showcases early Pallava and Chola architectural influences, serving as a silent witness to the city's multi-religious past.</li>
            <li><strong>The Vardhamana Temple:</strong> A smaller shrine within the precinct dedicated to Lord Mahavira. It is significant for its historic inscriptions that detail the land grants and royal patronage provided to the Jain community by various South Indian dynasties.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section>
        <h2>Sriperumbudur–Oragadam Corridor / ஸ்ரீபெரும்புதூர்-ஒரகடம் மாவட்டம்</h2>
        <p>If the heritage core is a ‘hand-crafted’ economy, this belt is a ‘mass-production’ monster. Located 30–40 km east of the city, this corridor is the reason Kanchipuram District is one of the wealthiest in India.<p>
	<p>The main economic driver of Kanchipuram is automobile, electronics, and aerospace manufacturing. The massive SIPCOT industrial parks housing Fortune 500 companies.<p>
	<p>Key manufacturers here include Hyundai, Renault-Nissan, Daimler (BharatBenz), Apollo Tyres, Samsung, Dell, and Foxconn (Apple’s primary assembler), and Saint-Gobain, drive the district’s GDP.</p>
    </section>

    <hr>

    <section>
        <h2>The Complete Journey</h2>
        <p>Start your day at the Kamakshi Temple at dawn for the spiritual experience, spend the afternoon in Shiva Kanchi watching silk being dyed and woven, and then take the drive toward Chennai in the evening to see the sprawling, glowing ‘city of factories’ in Sriperumbudur.<p>
	<p>It provides a jarring but essential view of how India balances its 10th-century traditions with its 21st-century ambitions.</p>
    </section>

